00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:37.390
So I share this story
with you because I

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:40.600
think it's consistent
with the experience I've

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:42.220
heard from a lot
of our customers

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:43.678
we've been working
with, as they've

00:21:43.678 --> 00:21:46.240
tried to take high performance
applications from how they're

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:49.030
running on-prem today
and into the cloud.

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:52.060
And we get really excited
about the engine, the tools,

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:54.460
the preemptible
VMs, and containers,

00:21:54.460 --> 00:21:57.070
and automatic scheduling,
all this great stuff

00:21:57.070 --> 00:21:58.570
that can happen in the cloud.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.570
And often, we get there
and we get started,

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:04.020
and then we figure out,
my tools in my garage

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:07.800
aren't quite compatible with
what's out there in the cloud.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:09.593
So we came together,
and this is really

00:22:09.593 --> 00:22:11.010
what we've been
working on jointly

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.080
with Google Cloud,
with the SchedMD folks,

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:14.490
with Slurm, and Elastifile.

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.580
We're really here to take a
lot of that complexity out

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:19.440
of the equation,
and try to shorten

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.690
the gap between
what you do today

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.100
and what you might do in Cloud.

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:24.850
And so we want to show
you how this works.

00:22:24.850 --> 00:22:28.980
We put together a demo that's
sort of simply architected

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:29.490
like this.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:32.220
It's running in a Google
project completely.

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:34.210
Slurm will be stood
up, we're going

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:35.680
to deploy a whole bunch of VMs.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:37.890
Preemptible VMs, actually.

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.680
We'll use Elastifile for
the back end storage,

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:42.960
and we actually have an
application that we authored

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:47.400
called E-run, which does a
pretty intense type of storage

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:48.540
load generation.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.990
And we're going to show
you what happens as we go.

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:54.300
OK, so I want to have Brian
come up, and he's going to--

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:55.710
actually, not yet.

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:57.330
So let me break
down the components,

00:22:57.330 --> 00:22:59.440
real quickly for you,
that are going to happen.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:01.560
So on the Elastifile side,
if you don't know us,

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:03.900
what we are is,
essentially, a scale-out

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:06.900
file system that's designed
to operate, essentially,

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:10.530
natively inside of
Google Cloud, OK?

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:12.990
It presents as an NFS server.

00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:13.900
Really simple.

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:15.810
No client-side software
needed, standard

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.720
NFS you've been using
for 10, 20, 30 years.

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:22.440
But on the back end, it's a
highly distributed file system

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:25.200
that handles embarrassingly
parallel connections really

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:26.340
well.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.380
It runs completely
inside of Google Cloud.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.150
So any region, any zone.

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:34.050
It can stand up and run in
a mere matter of minutes.

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:37.590
The system, like I
said, it's NSF v3.

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:38.730
It's designed to handle--

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.380
it's scale-out, so as
you need more capacity,

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:42.390
you scale horizontally.

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:44.755
You need more performance,
you scale horizontally.

00:23:44.755 --> 00:23:47.130
And then when you're done with
that performance capacity,

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:49.290
you can shrink it back
in, which actually works.

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.910
It's elastics in and out.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:55.860
So it's designed to get to large
capacities integrated for--

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.390
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:59.010
So it can tier.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:00.750
As you-- as your
data gets cooler,

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:02.520
and you don't want to move
it into a different place,

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:03.562
you can just cool it off.

00:24:03.562 --> 00:24:06.870
It'll push the inactive
files into Cloud Storage,

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:09.060
but keep the entire
namespace populated,

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:12.820
so the applications
still see all their data.

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:15.430
It's a highly available system,
if that's important to you.

00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:17.130
You can either run
it in a single zone,

00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:19.680
or many zones in a region,
or all the zones in a region,

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:23.640
and even do asynchronous
replication across zones.

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:26.910
Upgrades are automatic
and non-disruptive.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:29.280
And then tiering, as I said,
we can tier active data

00:24:29.280 --> 00:24:30.450
into Cloud Storage.

00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:32.820
You can also take
snapshots of data

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:35.760
and store them directly into
Google Cloud Storage, which

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:38.670
is a great thing if you
use storage on premises.

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:40.950
The traditional
filers, often, you

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:44.220
have to reserve a big chunk
of your data for redundancy,

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:47.460
and another big chunk of
your capacity for snapshots.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:50.260
In this case, we can keep all
of the performance capacity,

00:24:50.260 --> 00:24:52.470
which, in our case, runs
completely in Google Cloud

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:55.500
Engine, and then any
data for snapshots

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:57.780
can be stored in a
GCS at a lower cost,

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:01.690
and keep all of your primary
tier available for performance.

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:03.240
Lastly, there is a
capability called

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.580
Cloud Connect, which is a
data shipper for moving data

00:25:05.580 --> 00:25:06.870
in and out of Cloud.

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:08.250
This helps solve
some of the gap.

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:10.590
When I have a large
data set on-prem,

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:12.940
and I'm trying to figure out
how to get it in the cloud,

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:14.010
we can move all of that.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:18.000
And we keep all the POSIX stuff
as POSIX, mtime, atime, ctime,

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.470
ACLs, any of the metadata.

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:22.590
And so, even to
the point where we

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:25.110
can capture hard
links and soft links,

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:26.890
essentially take a
full snapshot of a data

00:25:26.890 --> 00:25:30.060
set, drop it in Google,
and start operating, OK?

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:31.630
So you have two options.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:34.530
You can deploy this in your
projects, you manage it,

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:35.580
we support it.

00:25:35.580 --> 00:25:38.580
Or fully managed service,
which is effectively

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:42.250
gone to a self-service
mode over the last week.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:44.490
Both of these accessible
through Google Marketplace.

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.350
The QR codes will get you there.

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:48.810
Click the link,
hit Go, and you're

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:50.610
up and running
within a few minutes.

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:52.230
As a matter of
fact, Brian, who's

00:25:52.230 --> 00:25:56.250
going to come up now
and talk about Slurm, he

00:25:56.250 --> 00:25:58.740
actually, during the
demo, used the service

00:25:58.740 --> 00:26:00.393
to do to do the implementation.

00:26:00.393 --> 00:26:01.560
So let me hand off to Brian.

00:26:04.012 --> 00:26:04.970
BRIAN CHRISTIANSEN: OK.

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:05.840
Thanks, Dave.

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:07.410
So I'm Brian Christiansen.

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:08.390
I'm with SchedMD.

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:13.190
We are the maintainers of the
open source workload manager

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:15.893
slash job scheduler.

00:26:15.893 --> 00:26:17.060
So a little bit about Slurm.

00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:20.690
Traditionally, we
find Slurm in the--

00:26:20.690 --> 00:26:24.980
more on the on-premise side.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:33.350
So, for example, in Slurm's on
five of the top 10 biggest HPC

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:34.950
systems in the world.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:38.510
And then also, when we're
going up into the top 100,

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:42.620
we're on 40% the top 100.

00:26:42.620 --> 00:26:45.950
So Slurm's scheduling a
wide range of use cases,

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:49.220
going from capability
to capacity machines,

00:26:49.220 --> 00:26:53.780
and also scheduling a
hybrid of those workloads.

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:55.880
For example, we've
got a customer

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.660
that does 14,000 jobs a minute
in high throughput environment.

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:05.870
So it's a little bit
about Slurm in the cloud

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:07.440
and where does it fit.

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:09.840
So a couple options of having--

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.380
of Slurm in the
cloud is one, you

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:17.420
could have Slurm as a
standalone clustered all in GCP.

00:27:17.420 --> 00:27:20.480
So you have your controller,
your login nodes,

00:27:20.480 --> 00:27:25.310
and all your compute
nodes, and then

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:28.140
what Slurm has
auto-scaling capability,

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:34.440
so it'll automatically create
and delete VM instances and GCP

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:36.480
according to the workload.

00:27:36.480 --> 00:27:37.930
And then also--

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:40.230
So if you've got an
on-premise system,

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:42.840
you could just
burst out into GCP,

00:27:42.840 --> 00:27:45.090
just actually
compute nodes there,

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:48.420
and using the auto-scaling
capabilities there.

00:27:48.420 --> 00:27:50.400
And then a hybrid
solution of the two

00:27:50.400 --> 00:27:54.450
is having your on-premise
cluster and a cluster

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:57.330
in the cloud, and beyond
those scheduled jobs,

00:27:57.330 --> 00:28:00.260
submit jobs between the two.

00:28:00.260 --> 00:28:02.167
And so we've worked with--

00:28:02.167 --> 00:28:03.750
we've been working
with Google to make

00:28:03.750 --> 00:28:10.830
it easy to set up a Slurm
cluster in the cloud easier,

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:17.940
and that's this repo,
the Slurm GCP repo.

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.640
So we're going to jump into the
demo that Dave and I created.

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:27.783
So as Dave explained, the demo--

00:28:27.783 --> 00:28:29.200
in the demo, we're
going to set up

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:32.050
an Elastifile cluster,
a Slurm cluster,

00:28:32.050 --> 00:28:35.140
and submit a whole bunch
of jobs and watch them run.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:39.190
So we'll show how
easy it is to do.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:41.420
OK, so this is the
Elastifile as a service.

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:43.670
We're going to go
ahead and create that

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:45.432
from the marketplace.

00:28:45.432 --> 00:28:46.640
We're just going to give it--

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:50.220
go ahead and give it a name,
choose our region and the zone.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:53.180
We're going to
head into the East.

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:57.560
And then there's some profiles
that just kind of give you

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:59.600
some base options.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:02.500
How much capacity.

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:05.960
And then we're going
to choose the network

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:08.270
that it's going to
reside in, so we're

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:10.440
going to do the default one.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:11.685
So we're going to head--

00:29:11.685 --> 00:29:13.310
so that's going to
go ahead and create.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:14.510
We're going to spin it up.

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:17.390
And the important thing that
we're looking for right now

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:18.440
is this share name.

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:20.450
This is the mount
point that we're

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.910
going to mount inside
of our instances.

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:25.272
So Elastifile's done.

00:29:25.272 --> 00:29:27.480
We're going to go ahead and
create our Slurm cluster.

00:29:27.480 --> 00:29:29.720
We're going to go to the
repo that we just saw,

00:29:29.720 --> 00:29:35.960
clone it, and then set up
the configuration file.

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:38.320
And inside the
configuration file--

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:41.220
so I'm going to set it up so
that we've got 1,000 nodes

00:29:41.220 --> 00:29:43.380
that we can burst out into.

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:46.110
And since the Elastifile
cluster's in the East,

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:49.890
we're going to go ahead
and put the cluster there.

00:29:49.890 --> 00:29:53.040
And our network was
the default one.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:56.130
If you don't do the default,
the Deployment Manager

00:29:56.130 --> 00:29:58.570
will actually create
its own network for it.

00:29:58.570 --> 00:30:01.500
And we're going to do eight
just to get a bigger bandwidth.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.310
And preemptible bursting, that's
so that any node that gets

00:30:04.310 --> 00:30:08.100
bursted out to is going
to be a preemptible VM.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:12.240
And then this is just
for a Slurm accounting

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:18.270
so that will run my jobs using
my email address when I SSH in.

00:30:18.270 --> 00:30:20.960
OK, so we're going
to do a little bit--

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:22.580
just a little bit more setup.

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:27.050
So taking that mount
point, we're going to--

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:30.650
this is a script that's
going to mount the Elastifile

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:35.010
service inside the instances and
install some things for them.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.340
The erun that Dave
talked about, this

00:30:37.340 --> 00:30:40.870
is the load generator
created by Elastifile.

00:30:43.503 --> 00:30:45.420
And then we're going to
go ahead and copy that

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:49.792
into the deployment
scripts, and so when

00:30:49.792 --> 00:30:51.750
we create the deployment
they'll get pushed up.

00:30:56.710 --> 00:31:02.390
OK, so we use Deployment
Manager to create the instances.

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:04.140
You're going to see
in Deployment Manager,

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:09.660
we created a router, an
instance for the controller,

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:12.360
an instance for the login
node, and you'll also

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:15.510
see a instance that is a G--

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:17.760
that is the compute image.

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:20.400
So what the compute
image is, is that it's

00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:22.860
going to be a
dummy compute node,

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.760
and once it gets
installed, then we're

00:31:25.760 --> 00:31:30.270
going to make a snapshot of
itself, create an image of it.

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:31.770
And then anytime
we burst out, we're

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:33.990
going to use that image
so that we can burst--

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:35.630
we can start up
VMs a lot quicker.

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:40.950
So I'm just logging
into the controller.

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:42.680
We'll see how the make installs.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:46.440
On-- down here on the bottom
right, I'm SCP'ing up,

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:50.740
just the script to do the erun.

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:51.770
And my tmux.conf.

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:59.418
OK, once that's-- once
that's finished installing,

00:31:59.418 --> 00:32:01.960
I'm just going out to the login
node where we're going to run

00:32:01.960 --> 00:32:07.210
the job from, and just set up
some monitoring on the side.

00:32:07.210 --> 00:32:11.160
But on the-- so
here, on the right,

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:14.740
I'm going to go into
the images page,

00:32:14.740 --> 00:32:20.900
and you'll see that the
compute image is creating.

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:23.310
Once that is completed--

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:27.690
So while it's building--
until it's done completing,

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:30.380
Slurm has marked
the controller--

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:34.130
the queue or the partition
is in a downstate

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:35.810
so no jobs can run on it.

00:32:35.810 --> 00:32:39.560
But we see that nodes are up--

00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:41.750
We see that Slurm's
available to run jobs,

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:46.190
but it's not in a state to
run run them yet until those

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:48.260
compute image finishes.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:52.520
Once it finishes, it calls
home and marks the queue

00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:55.610
in the partition in an
upstate so it can run jobs.

00:32:55.610 --> 00:33:01.580
And once it's up, the compute
image will delete itself

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:04.020
and will go away.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:08.120
So here, we see that
the Elastifile mount has

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:12.260
been mounted inside, at
least the login instance,

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:14.690
and it's been mounted
across everywhere.

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:17.510
So right here, I'm
submitting a job that's

00:33:17.510 --> 00:33:19.110
going to run on 10 nodes.

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:22.620
And it's just going run
hostname on 10 different nodes.

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:25.620
And it takes about 40--

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:28.860
49 seconds to finish there.

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:32.613
And so this will scale out
about every 1,000 nodes,

00:33:32.613 --> 00:33:34.530
we have to back off a
little bit because we're

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:36.930
hitting API limits,
but we can spin up

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:41.850
about 5,000 nodes in 7 minutes.

00:33:41.850 --> 00:33:46.200
Here, I'm going to go ahead
and submit the erun script.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:49.560
And this is an array job
and it's going to be a--

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:52.920
it's-- basically,
it's 1,000 jobs.

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:55.105
We're going to spin
up jobs and each job

00:33:55.105 --> 00:33:56.730
is going to be on
its own compute node.

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:06.520
So we'll see all the nodes
have been fanned out,

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:07.430
they're starting up.

00:34:07.430 --> 00:34:11.429
We see all the compute
nodes being created.

00:34:11.429 --> 00:34:14.870
And on the left, we're
just waiting for them

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:16.580
to check in with Slurm.

00:34:16.580 --> 00:34:18.350
And once they check in--

00:34:18.350 --> 00:34:21.230
and while they're checking in,
Slurm is starting the jobs.

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:25.190
So you can see over here,
on the Elastifile graphs,

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:29.469
that they're starting
to filter in--

00:34:29.469 --> 00:34:32.639
starting to run.

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:33.810
And then we speed up.

00:34:33.810 --> 00:34:37.170
And what you're going to see
next is a couple of these VMs

00:34:37.170 --> 00:34:38.389
are going to get preempted.

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:42.630
Slurm is going to detect that
the VMs have been preempted,

00:34:42.630 --> 00:34:44.250
jobs are going to get requeued.

00:34:44.250 --> 00:34:47.170
Jobs that were on those
nodes got requeued,

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:51.780
and so they become eligible
to run after two minutes.

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.550
And then Slurm will either--

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:57.990
Slurm-- We go ahead and
restart the instances

00:34:57.990 --> 00:34:59.520
when they've been preempted.

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:01.680
And then these jobs are
eligible to either run

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:02.607
on the node that--

00:35:02.607 --> 00:35:04.440
it could run on that
note that was preempted

00:35:04.440 --> 00:35:06.510
or it could fan
out-- or it could

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:08.670
spawn more nodes to run on.

00:35:12.670 --> 00:35:14.820
So right here--

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:17.070
So right now,
we've run our jobs.

00:35:17.070 --> 00:35:19.850
There-- they were
10-minute jobs.

00:35:19.850 --> 00:35:22.250
And now we're going
into the tail of it.

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:25.095
And this is just finishing
off the preemptibles--

00:35:27.770 --> 00:35:29.930
the preemptible jobs.

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:32.180
And then what we're
also going to see,

00:35:32.180 --> 00:35:33.750
too, is all these
compute nodes are

00:35:33.750 --> 00:35:35.300
going to clean them self off.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:39.640
After they've been idle for
a default of five minutes,

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:41.420
Slurm will start
tearing them down.

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:54.370
So we spun up 1,000
nodes and they all

00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:56.410
checked in within
about three minutes,

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:59.610
and they completed the--

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:01.420
and then the
preemptibles tailed off.

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:13.810
So pretty simple to set up an
Elastifile cluster and a Slurm

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:16.025
cluster in GCP.

00:36:16.025 --> 00:36:16.900
DAVE PAYNE: Right on.

00:36:16.900 --> 00:36:21.130
And actually, Brian did all that
work pretty much by himself.

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:24.110
When we start recording the
demo, he went and grabbed--

00:36:24.110 --> 00:36:25.750
I showed him how
to find Elastifile,

00:36:25.750 --> 00:36:28.333
and then he came back and said,
OK, I already built all of it,

00:36:28.333 --> 00:36:29.390
it all works.

00:36:29.390 --> 00:36:33.800
So a testament of an end
user to a large extent.

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:35.860
So essentially what we
just saw, to recap that,

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.450
is, as Brian said,
three minutes,

00:36:37.450 --> 00:36:40.850
we had 1,000 jobs queued and
another 1,000 machines created.

00:36:40.850 --> 00:36:42.700
And as you saw as
they showed up,

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:47.200
work was starting to fly through
the system, so super fast.

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:50.290
And even at that job, we
sped it up a little bit,

00:36:50.290 --> 00:36:51.310
for time's sake.

00:36:51.310 --> 00:36:53.230
No one wants to
watch a cake cooking.

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:55.300
But in that short
amount of time,

00:36:55.300 --> 00:36:57.730
I think it was up for 15
minutes, maybe, total,

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:01.270
from start to finish, about
1% of the 1,000 machines

00:37:01.270 --> 00:37:02.350
were preempted.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:05.620
And a preemptible machine
lasts for max 24 hours, right?

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:08.080
Whether it's a
GPU- or CPU-based.

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:11.330
But even still, in that short
amount of time, they were lost.

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:14.630
And so we did a session with--

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:19.810
an HPC session last year
with Silicon Therapeutics,

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:22.030
using GPUs that
were preemptible.

00:37:22.030 --> 00:37:24.260
And they didn't
have this feature.

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:26.590
You guys hadn't released your
feature, yet, with Slurm.

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:29.395
And so every day, he had to go
in and requeue all these jobs.

00:37:29.395 --> 00:37:33.610
It was a manual process to
find what broke and fix it all,

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:35.530
so that's a really great thing.

00:37:35.530 --> 00:37:37.840
And then most importantly,
if you've ever

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:39.820
been caught in the
cloud issue, which

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:43.150
is a month later I forgot to
turn off all these machines,

00:37:43.150 --> 00:37:44.500
Slurm prevents all of that.

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:45.250
It left no trace.

00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:47.250
As soon as the jobs are
idle, it cleaned them up

00:37:47.250 --> 00:37:50.680
and the billing stops
immediately, which is great.

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:52.150
Your accountants will like that.

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:55.120
So 1,000 machines spun
up, parallel NFS running,

00:37:55.120 --> 00:37:56.377
very simple configuration.

00:37:56.377 --> 00:37:58.210
He didn't have to install
any storage stuff.

00:37:58.210 --> 00:37:59.840
It just works out of the box.

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:01.600
We were sustaining
2 and 1/2 gigabytes

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:04.780
of sustained throughput
through the system,

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:07.380
and all of the data that
was written in those jobs

00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:10.900
was persisted even though,
when a preemptible goes down,

00:38:10.900 --> 00:38:12.970
the data gets deleted.

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:14.140
So that's the net of that.

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:16.000
We took preemptible
machines, which

00:38:16.000 --> 00:38:19.930
by nature are non-persistent and
unpredictable in when they're

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:24.780
going to go up and down, and we
eliminated the unpredictability

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:27.740
of the non-persistence of data.

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:31.480
We added Slurm and added
this extra predictability in,

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:35.590
sort of, accounting cleanup so
we could deliver reliability,

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:37.900
predictability for HPC.

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:41.897
Lastly, the question is, hey,
great, but what's the cost?

00:38:41.897 --> 00:38:43.480
And so we ran the
numbers to make sure

00:38:43.480 --> 00:38:45.070
that this is
actually achievable,

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:48.950
and essentially the cost
impact is negligible.

00:38:48.950 --> 00:38:52.670
If you look at the total cost
of running large HPC systems,

00:38:52.670 --> 00:38:54.650
most of it is compute oriented.

00:38:54.650 --> 00:38:58.690
So if you compare a standard
compute-based system

00:38:58.690 --> 00:39:01.990
to preemptibles, typically
are about-- they're about 80%

00:39:01.990 --> 00:39:03.220
less expensive.

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:06.790
You add in Slurm and Elastifile,
we did a model over a week

00:39:06.790 --> 00:39:10.960
and it was just a few percent
difference in the total price.

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:14.050
So your savings are
still 75% to 79%

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:17.480
over what a standard
deployment would look like.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:18.160
Michelle?

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:19.060
MICHELLE XU: Yeah, thank you.

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:19.500
Thank you.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:20.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:21.830
DAVE PAYNE: You're welcome.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:24.030
MICHELLE XU: Thank you, guys.

00:39:24.030 --> 00:39:25.600
Now we know how--

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:29.100
all know how to utilize the
cost savings of-- the beauty

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:32.580
of preemptible machines, as
well as getting our job done

00:39:32.580 --> 00:39:36.260
in a timely fashion.

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:39.410
And if you'd like to learn
more about Elastifile,

00:39:39.410 --> 00:39:40.770
take a photo of this.

00:39:40.770 --> 00:39:43.500
We'll have a session tomorrow.

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:44.240
All right.

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:45.398
Oh, sorry.

00:39:45.398 --> 00:39:45.940
There you go.

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:53.200
Let me wrap this session
with a user story.

00:39:53.200 --> 00:39:57.210
This user is extremely
special to us,

00:39:57.210 --> 00:39:59.970
because it is our own
engineering design

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:03.660
team, our own
semiconductor chip team.

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:06.600
And that's why we call the
story a Google on Google story.

00:40:09.670 --> 00:40:12.180
Although this is a Google
semiconductor chip engineering

00:40:12.180 --> 00:40:15.660
team, but our engineer
team resonates.

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:18.420
They have the same
problems, their design based

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:21.090
on same principles,
they use the same tools

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:24.460
as everybody else in the world.

00:40:24.460 --> 00:40:28.190
So in case you're not familiar
with semiconductor chip design,

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:31.230
you lay out a hardware
design, and then

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.140
you put in some boundary
conditions, input conditions,

00:40:34.140 --> 00:40:36.600
and you similarly
the design first.

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:39.690
When you're happy with the
final simulation results,

00:40:39.690 --> 00:40:41.730
you send that blueprint
to a manufacturer

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:43.560
to be manufactured.

00:40:43.560 --> 00:40:46.350
And then the manufacturers
send the sample chips back,

00:40:46.350 --> 00:40:48.750
and you measure them and
to see if the results are

00:40:48.750 --> 00:40:50.940
exactly how you design
it and the results

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:52.350
are what you're expecting.

00:40:52.350 --> 00:40:55.670
If not, likely you have to go
back and retweak the design,

00:40:55.670 --> 00:40:58.330
and send it back to the
factory, now probably beta,

00:40:58.330 --> 00:41:00.330
and then the factory sends
the sample chip back,

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:01.600
you measure it again.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:05.610
So this whole iteration
takes one year, two years,

00:41:05.610 --> 00:41:07.020
easily several years.

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:08.100
Easily.

00:41:08.100 --> 00:41:11.640
So our own chip design
team is thinking,

00:41:11.640 --> 00:41:17.010
there has to be a way where
we can reduce the engineering

00:41:17.010 --> 00:41:20.020
design cycle.

00:41:20.020 --> 00:41:25.060
And so they decided
to move to the cloud.

00:41:25.060 --> 00:41:30.280
And just like everybody else
in the world, they're thinking,

00:41:30.280 --> 00:41:33.520
we don't want to
refactor our workflow,

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:39.010
we do not want to redo our
system, and, at the same time,

00:41:39.010 --> 00:41:41.620
we want all of the
benefits from the cloud.

00:41:44.280 --> 00:41:47.810
So in the early stage of
their workload migration,

00:41:47.810 --> 00:41:51.680
they've decided that, OK, we'll
keep our interactive sessions

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:58.310
on prem, and we'll keep majority
of our repositories on prem.

00:41:58.310 --> 00:42:01.040
And now when it
comes to batch jobs,

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:02.915
we need to launch
thousands of them,

00:42:02.915 --> 00:42:04.790
and we don't even have
this amount of machine

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:07.400
on premises, so we'll
do that in the cloud.

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:14.990
And if you're familiar with
semiconductor chip design,

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:17.750
or if you have paid
attention to some

00:42:17.750 --> 00:42:19.300
of the jobs you have
ran in the past,

00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:23.480
you will notice that some
jobs require more CPUs,

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:27.450
some require more frequency,
some require more memory.

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:29.640
And so our own design
team, when they

00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:32.300
looked at the
profile of our jobs,

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:34.590
they decided to
use custom machine

00:42:34.590 --> 00:42:38.790
types, which is a product
that's available in Google Cloud

00:42:38.790 --> 00:42:39.750
today.

00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:42.210
So custom machine
types really is

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:45.304
a cost saving play so
you don't overprovision.

00:42:48.210 --> 00:42:49.680
And then they are
using containers,

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:54.190
because it's easier to
deploy, easy to manage.

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:58.750
And our own team is also, as
we just saw in the demo earlier

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:00.880
by Elastifile, as
well as Slurm, we

00:43:00.880 --> 00:43:03.010
saw that, if we're
able to manage

00:43:03.010 --> 00:43:06.880
preemptible VMs properly,
we can actually have

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:09.280
quite a bit of cost saving.

00:43:09.280 --> 00:43:15.930
And also be very efficient with
our engineering design cycle.

00:43:15.930 --> 00:43:19.350
And lastly, and probably
the most importantly,

00:43:19.350 --> 00:43:21.390
is security and compliance.

00:43:21.390 --> 00:43:25.320
Those hardware layouts
are extremely sensitive,

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:27.420
and our team has
the same concerns

00:43:27.420 --> 00:43:30.060
as everybody else in the world.

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:34.490
So I'll flash this very
high-level architectural design

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:37.940
that our own team's deployment
has today in Google Cloud.

00:43:37.940 --> 00:43:41.480
On the left is their
on-premises deployment.

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:45.140
It's standard compute, plus
some storage components

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:48.970
and it's connected to
Google Cloud through a VPN.

00:43:48.970 --> 00:43:51.320
And in their cloud,
they will have--

00:43:51.320 --> 00:43:52.910
they stage their jobs--

00:43:52.910 --> 00:43:54.320
when it comes to
job staging, you

00:43:54.320 --> 00:43:58.340
have your design files,
inputs, parameters, boundary

00:43:58.340 --> 00:44:02.090
conditions, all loaded
in cloud storage.

00:44:02.090 --> 00:44:04.250
And then they will have
different persistent disks

00:44:04.250 --> 00:44:07.880
to load different design tools.

00:44:07.880 --> 00:44:10.120
And then, eventually,
all the computation

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:14.240
is done in either custom
machine types, containers,

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:17.060
or just normal VMs that are
available on Google Cloud

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:18.620
today.

00:44:18.620 --> 00:44:21.350
And so this work was
actually done before the C2

00:44:21.350 --> 00:44:25.770
and M2s were announced or made
available to our customers.

00:44:25.770 --> 00:44:28.520
So the C2 are the
high frequency ones,

00:44:28.520 --> 00:44:31.310
the M2 is a 12 terabyte
machine, so I highly

00:44:31.310 --> 00:44:35.090
suspect our own team might go
in and try our own C2 and M2

00:44:35.090 --> 00:44:38.090
machines very soon.

00:44:38.090 --> 00:44:42.200
So that is all the content
we have today for everyone

00:44:42.200 --> 00:44:43.880
and thank you for joining us.

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:50.210
And so to recap, we are
no longer limited by what

00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:51.920
computers we have in our labs.

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:55.220
We're no longer limited by
where the computers are.

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:57.900
Time has changed.

00:44:57.900 --> 00:45:02.260
We're in the era where
engineers are empowered.

00:45:02.260 --> 00:45:05.450
We're more efficient
at what we do,

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:08.440
and so we can clear
room in our schedules

00:45:08.440 --> 00:45:11.810
so we can solve more
problems and make

00:45:11.810 --> 00:45:14.150
bigger impacts in the world.

00:45:14.150 --> 00:45:15.610
Thank you.

00:45:15.610 --> 00:45:20.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:20.360 --> 00:45:24.260
We have a HPC link here if you
want to check out the content

00:45:24.260 --> 00:45:27.170
and assets we have today.

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:30.820
